WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.500
Before you trust any chart, read its title block.

2
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:07.500
It takes a minute, and it tells you what all the numbers mean.

3
00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:10.550
The chart number, the title and the scale.

4
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.600
The units for soundings: feet, fathoms or meters.

5
00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:19.800
Get that one wrong and a six on the chart could be six feet, or thirty-six.

6
00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:25.200
And the depths are measured from a low-water level, usually mean lower low water.

7
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:30.875
The horizontal datum, normally the North American Datum of nineteen eighty-three.

8
00:00:31.125 --> 00:00:37.300
For practical purposes it matches the WGS eighty-four datum in your GPS.

9
00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:41.075
If the two don’t match, your plotted position is off.

10
00:00:41.325 --> 00:00:43.150
Heights are different.

11
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:48.700
Lights, landmarks and bridge clearances are measured above mean high water.

12
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:52.925
Why would depths use low water and bridges use high water?

13
00:00:53.925 --> 00:00:56.025
Both are the cautious choice.

14
00:00:56.275 --> 00:00:58.175
And the edition date.

15
00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:00.375
Keep the chart corrected.

16
00:01:00.625 --> 00:01:02.450
That’s lesson one.

17
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.550
Latitude first.

18
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.175
A minute of latitude is a mile.

19
00:01:07.425 --> 00:01:09.875
Large scale, large detail.

20
00:01:10.125 --> 00:01:12.625
Next time: reading the symbols.
